<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Captaincy General of Santo Domingo</h1><p class="st-meta">1493 CE – 1795 CE · capital Santo Domingo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's first colony in the Americas, on Hispaniola, from which the conquest of the Caribbean and mainland was launched.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-isabela">La Isabela</a> on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> in 1493, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> was established on the south coast in 1496-98. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-ovando">Nicolás de Ovando</a>, governor from 1502, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> chiefdoms were destroyed and the island became the base of Spanish power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> received the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> in 1511, the first cathedral and, in 1538, a university. Gold mining using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> forced labour gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranching worked by enslaved Africans. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain's</a> attention turned to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, the colony declined. The 'devastations' of 1605-06 depopulated the north and west to stop smuggling.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French buccaneers settled the empty west, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> recognised as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> in 1697. By the Peace of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-basel">Basel</a> in 1795 Spain ceded the rest of the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The former colony later became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/captaincy-general-of-santo-domingo.jpg" alt="Captaincy General of Santo Domingo" width="320" height="196"><figcaption>Image: Risantana · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_hispaniola_island_dominican_republic_haiti_3.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1493 CE – 1795 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santo Domingo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nicolás de Ovando, Diego Colón</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Spanish Santo Domingo, Audiencia of Santo Domingo, La Española</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1644&amp;focus=captaincy-general-of-santo-domingo" aria-label="Captaincy General of Santo Domingo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2785 1493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Captaincy General of Santo Domingo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3105 1749L4910 989M3105 1749L3105 1749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3105 1749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Santo Domingo. <a href="/atlas?year=1644&amp;focus=captaincy-general-of-santo-domingo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
